In our test automation framework, the test cases are organized as a day-wise journey (Day 1, Day 2, Day 3, and so on), and they must always be executed in sequence because later days depend on the previous ones.

For example, if I’m working on a Day 6 test case (e.g., LD6 RUGT BKF 1.6) and it has an issue, I can’t simply rerun that test. I first need to identify and execute all the prerequisite test cases from Day 1 through Day 5 in the correct order.

Currently, I have to manually refer to a Notepad++ file (shown in the attached image) to determine the execution sequence. We also have multiple test data folders, which makes this process even more cumbersome.

What is the best way to simplify this workflow? I’m looking for a solution where I don’t have to manually map the dependencies every time. Ideally, I want an easier way to automatically identify the required dependent test cases and execute them in the correct order before running the target test.
